## 8. Geometry requirements and scientific quality

Do not create fake anatomical completeness using spheres, tubes, capsules or arbitrary shapes.

Procedural geometry may be used for clearly disclosed educational approximations only when scientifically justified and reviewed. It must never be presented as a verified anatomical mesh.

Every new mesh must satisfy:

- Correct anatomical location.
- Anatomically plausible shape and orientation.
- Correct side and laterality.
- Correct scale.
- Appropriate anatomical relationships.
- No accidental overlap or penetration with adjacent structures.
- Valid triangle/index buffers.
- Finite vertices and bounds.
- Consistent normals and winding.
- Appropriate tissue classification and materials.
- Stable unique mesh ID.
- Correct ontology mapping.
- Correct system assignment.
- Correct selection and isolation behaviour.

Follow the existing coordinate convention:
- Y-up.
- Right-handed.
- Meters.
- Standing reference pose facing +Z.
- Feet near Y = 0.
- Head near the current reference height.

Respect existing model loading, chunking, optimization and LOD architecture.

Preserve source master geometry. Never overwrite original source files destructively.

For imported structures, retain source IDs and provenance even if ANATOMY 360 assigns its own internal IDs.

